new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ebe4c392554c50813e71680aae7df254371bc901
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,179 @@
+# arXiv 1611.01542 — verified p-set-splitter generator
+
+**Status: locally verified, but not shippable yet.** G1–G8 pass. The required
+four-vendor run and G9 arms could not obtain a single scored attempt because the
+configured OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` on
+every redraw. The script-owned error transcript is preserved; no hardness verdict
+has been invented.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | exact cover |
+| certificate form | integer tuple (the natural splitter subset) |
+| intended intuition | invariant: detect a modular arithmetic progression among regular-incidence decoys |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and construction
+
+This module instantiates the native definition from Bernstein, Bortner, Coskey,
+Li, and Simpson, [*The set splittability problem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01542).
+For `p = 1/d`, it gives the solver a finite collection in which every listed set
+has `d` elements. The solver must return a ground-set subset meeting every listed
+set exactly once. Verification is just exact set membership and intersection
+counting.
+
+The generator first constructs a simple 3-uniform `d`-regular incidence
+hypergraph with a perfect matching, labels that matching by a modular arithmetic
+progression, adds same-marginal random residue labels to all decoys, and shuffles
+the presentation. Degree-preserving switches remove the initial decoy-layer
+boundaries. The planted matching is retained throughout, so generation never
+solves the emitted instance.
+
+## Why Track B, and what is easy
+
+Section 2 formalizes p-SPLIT as `M y = round(p M 1)` for a binary incidence
+matrix, and Theorem 2.1 proves NP-completeness for each fixed `0 < p < 1`. That is
+only worst-case context; it is **not** claimed as average-case hardness for this
+distribution. A construction-aware algorithm does exist: scan every ordered pair
+of the `d*n` labels as a possible start and second term, generate `n` modular
+terms, and verify complete progressions. It costs `O((dn)^2 n)` exact operations.
+At the shipping target (`n=40,d=5`) the implementation performs exactly
+1,592,000 modular advances, solving 8/8 instances in 0.160 s mean and 0.207 s max
+on this machine. Once the hidden start and step are recognized, writing the
+progression needs only 39 modular additions. That mechanical-versus-compact gap
+is the Track B claim.
+
+The construction avoids the paper's explicit easy regimes. Theorem 3.3 makes
+all collections of at most two sets splittable; these instances have `3n` sets.
+Theorem 4.4 applies when every multiplicity-one Venn region is large; here every
+ground element has multiplicity exactly three. Theorem 4.5 concerns ground-set
+size in `omega(2^m m)` for `m` sets; here there are only `dn` elements and `3n`
+sets. The paper's small three- and four-set classifications therefore do not
+produce this certificate.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
+
+This smallest instance is genuinely hand-solvable: the four answer labels are
+an ordinary arithmetic progression with common difference 228163.
+
+```text
+Find an exact p-splitter for the finite set collection below.
+
+Definitions: the ground set U is the displayed set of distinct integer labels.
+For p = 1/d, a p-splitter is a subset S of U such that every listed set B
+contains exactly the nearest integer to |B|/d elements of S. Here every
+listed set has d = 3 elements, so the required intersection size is exactly 1.
+Every ground-set label occurs in exactly three listed sets; consequently S must
+have exactly n = 4 labels. Order does not matter and repeats are forbidden.
+
+prime = 1000003
+p = 1/3
+U = 6752, 52188, 238036, 449073, 466199, 540313, 561537, 691662,
+    694362, 777961, 900827, 922525
+B0 = 52188, 466199, 561537
+B1 = 6752, 52188, 922525
+B2 = 449073, 694362, 900827
+B3 = 52188, 900827, 922525
+B4 = 466199, 540313, 691662
+B5 = 6752, 466199, 777961
+B6 = 561537, 691662, 922525
+B7 = 449073, 694362, 900827
+B8 = 238036, 561537, 691662
+B9 = 540313, 694362, 777961
+B10 = 238036, 449073, 777961
+B11 = 6752, 238036, 540313
+```
+
+Answer: `<answer>238036, 466199, 694362, 922525</answer>`.
+`verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping the last label returns
+`(False, "wrong cardinality: expected 4 labels")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | n | d | universe | answer atoms | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 3 | 12 | 4 | hand example; harden.py skips it |
+| easy | 40 | 5 | 200 | 40 | current shipping target; oracle run blocked externally |
+| medium | 48 | 6 | 288 | 48 | available |
+| hard | 56 | 7 | 392 | 56 | available |
+
+Escalation first increases `d`, enlarging the decoy haystack without lengthening
+the answer. Only after that axis is exhausted does it raise `n`.
+
+## Gate measurements
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses verified |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | prose/fence/tag round-trip and JSON-native answer |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 uniform fixed-cardinality guesses; space `C(200,40)` = 2.050157995198589e42 |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; demo has exactly 1/495 solutions; reference cost 1,592,000 ops |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks at 0/8 each, including 256 restarts; reference progression scan 8/8 as Track B expects |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=80` verifies; fixed-answer escalation to `d=6` verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 40 relabelling invariance checks, 40 carried-witness checks, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 316 characters, 79 estimated tokens, 40 atoms, 39 intended modular additions |
+| G9(a,b) | **not run** | OpenRouter quota blocked all scored attempts; therefore G9 as a whole is not passed |
+
+The four failing G6 probes were incidence-frequency outlier selection, greedy
+first fit, 256 randomized greedy restarts, and the in-context ansatz that treats
+the first two printed labels as consecutive progression terms. Each scored 0/8.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare harness started at `easy`, but all four records were API errors and do
+not count as model failures. The script exited with “oracle pool is unreachable”
+and correctly wrote no verdict.
+
+| arm / model | seed | scored | outcome |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| bare / openai/gpt-5.6-terra | 1136750377 | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| bare / x-ai/grok-4.6 | 725833011 | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| bare / x-ai/grok-4.6 | 125187209 | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| bare / x-ai/grok-4.6 | 1891253641 | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| hinted | — | 0/0 | not attempted after bare pool failure |
+| placebo | — | 0/0 | not attempted after bare pool failure |
+
+Consequently `hinted - placebo` is not estimable, and no conclusion about the
+hint's causal value is claimed. After quota restoration, rerun bare first, then
+the structural and placebo copies in separate scratch directories as required.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_1611_01542 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+text = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + ", ".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after valid oracle/G9 evidence exists:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1611.01542 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a Track B representation puzzle, not evidence of average-case
+NP-hardness. Numeric residue labels add a construction-aware shortcut not present
+in arbitrary instances from the paper; the verifier itself uses only the native
+set-splitting relation. The 0/200,000 density result is empirical under the stated
+uniform prior over all 40-subsets—it is not an exact solution count or a confidence
+proof that the true fraction is zero. The successful reference scan makes the
+family easy with a sandbox, and a more optimized progression detector would use
+fewer operations than the deliberately direct full scan.
+
+The canonical key is a strong invariant (rooted closed walks and local
+codegrees), not a complete canonical form for bipartite incidence isomorphism;
+rare nonisomorphic collisions remain possible. No SAT/ILP package, DLX library,
+or algebraic attack beyond the documented exact-cover heuristics was available
+or run. Most importantly, the oracle and hinted G9 gates remain unmeasured because
+of the external quota failure, so this directory must not be submitted as a
+finished family yet.
